        <div class="docs-content">
            <section class="docs-section" id="whats-new">
                <h2 class="docs-section-title">What's new — July 2026</h2>
                <p>
                    A summary of recent functionality across the app. All database changes shipped as additive
                    migrations that apply automatically on the next deploy.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Estimate → Sales order → Production pipeline</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>Notes carry through conversion</strong> — the Convert form pre-fills order notes and per-line notes from the estimate (previously dropped).</li>
                    <li><strong>Sales rep carries to the sales order</strong> — set from the estimate, shown on the order page; historical orders backfilled.</li>
                    <li><strong>Quoted description carries</strong> — the estimate line's reworded description is snapshotted on sales order lines and shown on order pages, job screens, the ticket, and invoice lines; backfilled.</li>
                    <li><strong>Convert page validation</strong> — a quantity tier must be chosen per line (no silent top-break default); Customer PO and ship date required.</li>
                    <li><strong>Unwind direction</strong> — selectable per estimate line and per sales order line (standard #1–#8 chart), carried through the spec snapshot to jobs, the job page, and the ticket.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Sales order editing</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>Redesigned order page</strong> — dense Order/Shipping cards, related jobs/shipments/invoices and the order total in a side rail.</li>
                    <li><strong>Full line spec editor</strong> — every estimate-level attribute is editable per line: size, corner radius, gutters, bleed, substrate, die, ink set, white/spot colors, finishing operations with materials, waste, imposition overrides, and unwind.</li>
                    <li><strong>Unlock in-production orders</strong> — Admin/CSR can unlock a locked order, edit any detail, and save; spec and quantity changes flow to the linked jobs in place. Lines with jobs keep their product; lines without jobs stay fully editable.</li>
                    <li><strong>Exported-invoice warning</strong> — editing an order whose invoice was already exported to QuickBooks shows a red alert naming the invoice and export date.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Estimating engine &amp; form</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>CMYKW $0 click bug fixed</strong> — CMYK-family ink sets without their own non-spot ink row fall back to the CMYK process click rate.</li>
                    <li><strong>Per-quantity margins</strong> — each quantity break has its own margin control; the line-level control sets all; persisted per break.</li>
                    <li><strong>Lamination/foil material costing</strong> — those finishing types take an assigned laminate stock, charge its consumption (MSI × cost), and the stock choice rides the spec into the sales order and job.</li>
                    <li><strong>UX fixes</strong> — the calculation breakdown keeps expanded sections through recalcs; quantity reordering runs on a visible 3-second countdown; the estimate email is saved with the estimate, re-editable, with a customer-contacts picker in the send modal.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Jobs &amp; the production floor</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>Job page redesigned for operators</strong> — everything above the fold: collapsible artwork viewer (expandable in-browser PDF preview, file name in the header), current-step panel, dense job facts (SKU, size, die, ink, substrate, unwind, line notes, ship-by), operations and material usage.</li>
                    <li><strong>Production counts</strong> — one <em>Record &amp; mark …</em> button per stage records good/waste/downtime for that stage's operation <em>and</em> advances the job, so a stage can't be completed without its numbers. Fields prefill with expected counts (planned quantity, spec waste) or the previously recorded values for adjustments.</li>
                    <li><strong>Order context</strong> — the job page lists all sibling jobs on the sales order with statuses and links; the jobs list shows each job's order number.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Sales order ticket (job ticket)</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>Full redesign as a sales order ticket</strong> — company logo/name from General Settings, a scannable Code 128 of the sales order number up top, an order-level banner (due, ship date, total qty, jobs, PO), order + line notes before the job blocks.</li>
                    <li><strong>A compact block for every job on the order</strong> — description, size, die, substrate, ink, quantities, status/schedule, finishing tasks with materials, packaging, unwind, spec, and line notes — each with its own small scannable job barcode.</li>
                    <li><strong>Route with operator sign-off columns</strong> and the printed job's notes remain specific to the printed job.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">PDFs</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>All PDF generation moved to the in-house PdfPlatform library</strong> (estimates, purchase orders, tickets) — QuestPDF removed. Includes QR and 1D barcode (Code 128 et al.) support.</li>
                    <li><strong>Artwork keeps its original file name</strong> — shown on the job page and sales order lines, and used for downloads instead of the timestamped storage key.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Shipping</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>Mark shipped without packages</strong> — a one-click action on the Ready to Ship tab (with confirmation) for hand-offs that have no packages or tracking; reads <em>Mark picked up</em> for pickup-method orders. Only enabled when every item on the order is ready.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Invoices &amp; QuickBooks</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>Ready to export / Exported tabs</strong> — invoices track who exported them and when; per-row and checkbox bulk actions to mark exported, export selected to a QuickBooks CSV, or unmark.</li>
                    <li><strong>Sales tax rate</strong> moved from configuration into <a asp-page="/Settings/General">General settings</a>.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Finance &amp; access</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>Dashboard is now purely operational</strong> — financial widgets moved to the new <a asp-page="/Reports/Index">Finance reports</a> page (revenue, receivables, AR aging, order intake, top customers) with an ad-hoc report generator (invoices, payments, orders, estimates, jobs, shipments, POs; date + customer/product/die/supplier filters; CSV export).</li>
                    <li><strong>Estimate vs Actual</strong> — a job-costing page comparing quoted cost against recorded labor + material, with variance and margin per job, charts, filters, and CSV export.</li>
                    <li><strong>Access</strong> — the entire Finance area (invoices, reports, EVA, AR aging) requires the <strong>Accounting</strong> role explicitly; assign it alongside other roles under Setup → Users.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Dies, stocks, customers</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>Die entity</strong> — labels across is user-entered (formula validation removed); dimension fields display 4 decimals consistently.</li>
                    <li><strong>Form layouts</strong> — dies, stocks, and customers reorganized into labeled fieldsets; customer address/contact lists compacted to two-line cards with corner ✕ remove.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Pagination</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>Fixed and unified</strong> — corrected swapped page/total arguments ("page 31 of 25"), and every list now uses the shared pager: First/Prev/Next/Last plus a window of up to 10 page links around the current page, carrying all active filters. Products (previously unpaged) and sales orders (previously hand-rolled) included.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section" id="master-data-overview">
                <h2 class="docs-section-title">Master data overview</h2>
                <p>
                    Master data records are long-lived reference data. They are soft-deleted (marked inactive)
                    rather than removed, so historical estimates and jobs keep valid references.
                </p>
                <div class="docs-callout docs-callout--tip">
                    <strong>Recommended order</strong>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        Set up suppliers first, then stocks (which link to suppliers), then customers and dies.
                        Configure presses, inks, and finishing operations before creating estimates.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section" id="customers">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Customers</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/Customers/Index">Open customers</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    Customers are the billing and shipping parties for estimates, sales orders, and invoices.
                    Each customer can have multiple addresses and contacts.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Setup</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Create a unique <strong>code</strong> and display <strong>name</strong> for each account.</li>
                    <li>Set <strong>status</strong> (prospect, active customer, etc.) and payment <strong>terms</strong>.</li>
                    <li>Enter <strong>default markup %</strong> if this customer uses a standard margin on estimates.</li>
                    <li>Add at least one <strong>billing</strong> and one <strong>shipping</strong> address when you will ship or invoice.</li>
                    <li>Assign a <strong>sales rep</strong> and primary <strong>contact</strong> when applicable.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Usage</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Selected on every <strong>estimate</strong> and carried through to sales orders and jobs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Tax exempt</strong> flag affects whether tax is calculated on quotes and invoices.</li>
                    <li><strong>Customer-owned dies</strong> link back to the customer record for tooling traceability.</li>
                    <li>Deactivate instead of deleting when an account is no longer active — past transactions remain intact.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section" id="suppliers">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Suppliers</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/Suppliers/Index">Open suppliers</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    Suppliers are vendors for stock, dies, and other purchased materials. Stock records
                    require a supplier; dies can reference both a supplier and a customer (for customer-owned tooling).
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Setup</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Assign a short <strong>code</strong> and full <strong>name</strong> (e.g. Avery, RotoMetrics).</li>
                    <li>Record <strong>terms</strong>, <strong>account number</strong>, and <strong>default lead time</strong> in days.</li>
                    <li>Add one or more vendor <strong>contacts</strong> for purchasing and reorder follow-up. Use <strong>+ Add contact</strong> to add rows and <strong>Remove</strong> to drop them; existing contacts stay editable when you edit the supplier. Mark one as the <strong>primary contact</strong>.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Usage</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Linked from <strong>stock</strong> records for cost and reorder context.</li>
                    <li>Referenced on <strong>dies</strong> to track who manufactured the tool.</li>
                    <li>Used on <strong>purchase orders</strong> when ordering material from a vendor.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section" id="stocks">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Stocks</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/Stocks/Index">Open stocks</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    Stocks (substrates) describe face material, adhesive, liner, roll width, and cost.
                    The estimating engine uses stock width and cost per MSI to calculate material usage.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Setup</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Enter <strong>code</strong> and <strong>description</strong> that match how the shop orders material.</li>
                    <li>Record <strong>face material</strong>, <strong>adhesive</strong>, and <strong>liner</strong> for spec clarity.</li>
                    <li>Set <strong>width (in)</strong> to the purchased roll width and <strong>total caliper (mil)</strong> when known.</li>
                    <li>Link the <strong>supplier</strong>, <strong>supplier part number</strong>, and <strong>cost per MSI</strong>.</li>
                    <li>Enter <strong>minimum order quantity (LF)</strong> for purchasing guidance.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Usage</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Selected on estimate lines; drives imposition width and material cost in calculations.</li>
                    <li>Received on <strong>rolls</strong> via purchase orders for inventory tracking.</li>
                    <li>MSI (thousand square inches) is the standard North American pricing unit for label stock.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section" id="dies">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Dies</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/Dies/Index">Open dies</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    Dies define label shape and layout on the web: dimensions across and around, gutters,
                    labels per repeat, and die type. They anchor imposition when a die is specified on an estimate.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Setup</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Enter <strong>label across</strong>, <strong>label around</strong>, and <strong>corner radius</strong> in inches.</li>
                    <li>Set <strong>labels around</strong>, <strong>gutter</strong> dimensions, and <strong>web width</strong> for the tool layout.</li>
                    <li>Choose <strong>die type</strong> (flexible, solid, etc.) and optional <strong>shape</strong> description.</li>
                    <li>Link <strong>customer</strong> when the die is customer-owned; link <strong>supplier</strong> and part number for reorder.</li>
                    <li>Record <strong>location</strong> in the shop for physical retrieval.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Usage</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Optional on estimates; when selected, imposition uses die layout instead of manual label counts.</li>
                    <li>Supports tooling traceability for quality complaints and regulated industries.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section" id="inks">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Inks</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/Inks/Index">Open inks</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    Ink records are <strong>pricing rows</strong>, not individual color stations. An estimate line
                    picks an <strong>ink set</strong> (CMYK, EPM, CMYK+Spot, CMYKW, CMYKW+Spot) plus optional
                    <strong>white</strong> hits and <strong>spot colors</strong>. The engine looks up the matching ink
                    rows to get click rates and ink-consumption costs. You do <strong>not</strong> create separate
                    Cyan / Magenta / Yellow / Black records.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Setup</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Create <strong>one process row per ink set you quote</strong> (e.g. one row with ink set
                        <strong>CMYK</strong>, <strong>Spot color</strong> off, carrying the press
                        <strong>click rate per 1,000</strong>). The separation count (CMYK = 4, EPM = 3) is applied
                        automatically — you do not create four rows for the four colors.</li>
                    <li>Create <strong>one row per spot color</strong>: check <strong>Spot color</strong> and pick the
                        <strong>color</strong> (White, Silver, Green, Orange, Red, Blue, Violet). White is the special
                        spot tied to the <strong>W</strong> in the ink set; every other color (including silver) is a
                        selectable spot.</li>
                    <li>Process color is billed as <strong>clicks only</strong> — leave the bottle/coverage fields at 0.</li>
                    <li>For spot consumption costing, set <strong>bottle cost</strong>, <strong>bottle size (mL)</strong>,
                        <strong>mL per 1,000 in²</strong>, and <strong>default coverage %</strong>. Leave mL per 1,000 in²
                        at 0 to charge clicks only. These fields appear only when <strong>Spot color</strong> is checked.</li>
                    <li>On spot rows, optionally set <strong>press speed overrides (fpm)</strong> for 1, 2, and 3 hits.
                        These fields appear only when <strong>Spot color</strong> is checked; leave blank for no slowdown.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warning">
                    <strong>Critical: how rows are matched</strong>
                    <ul class="docs-list mb-0">
                        <li><strong>Process color</strong> is matched by <em>ink set + not-spot</em>. The
                            <strong>White</strong> control is matched <strong>only by the spot color White</strong> — its
                            ink set is ignored, so one white row covers every ink set.</li>
                        <li>Other <strong>spot colors</strong> are matched by the <strong>specific row you pick</strong>
                            in the estimate's spot dropdown.</li>
                        <li>Only the <strong>first active match by code (alphabetical)</strong> is used for process and
                            white. Do <strong>not</strong> create two process rows for the same set or two White rows —
                            duplicates are silently ignored and the alphabetically-first code wins.</li>
                        <li>If <strong>no matching row exists</strong>, the click rate falls back to <strong>$0</strong>
                            with no warning, silently under-pricing the quote. Ink rows are <strong>not seeded</strong> —
                            create them before estimating.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Usage</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>The estimate line's <strong>ink set</strong> drives process click cost
                        (impressions × frame slots × separations × click rate).</li>
                    <li><strong>White hits</strong> (0–3) and coverage % appear on <strong>CMYKW</strong> and
                        <strong>CMYKW+Spot</strong> lines and add white on top of process.</li>
                    <li>A repeating <strong>Spot colors</strong> picker appears on <strong>CMYK+Spot</strong> and
                        <strong>CMYKW+Spot</strong> lines: choose a configured spot color, set its hits (1–3) and
                        coverage %, and use <strong>+ Add spot</strong> for more. Each spot is priced just like white.</li>
                    <li>Each white/spot hit adds a click charge; configured bottle fields add an
                        <strong>mL ink-consumption</strong> cost on top.</li>
                    <li>If a white or spot row has a press speed override for the selected hit count, the estimate's
                        press run uses the <strong>slowest applicable speed</strong> (the slowest of the base press
                        speed and any white/spot override), increasing press run time and cost.</li>
                    <li><strong>EPM</strong> (Enhanced Productivity Mode) is its own ink set (3 separations) — create an
                        EPM row if you quote it.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section" id="finishing-operations">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Finishing operations</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/FinishingOperations/Index">Open finishing operations</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    Finishing operations are off-press steps such as laminating, slitting, rewinding, or die-cutting
                    on a separate finisher. Each operation has default setup time, run speed, and hourly cost.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Setup</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Define a unique <strong>code</strong> and clear <strong>description</strong>.</li>
                    <li>Choose <strong>operation type</strong> (laminate, slit, rewind, etc.).</li>
                    <li>Enter <strong>equipment name</strong>, <strong>default setup minutes</strong>, and <strong>default run speed (fpm)</strong>.</li>
                    <li>Set <strong>cost per hour</strong> for labor and overhead recovery in estimates and jobs.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Usage</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Attached to estimate lines; setup and run times roll into total production minutes.</li>
                    <li>Per-line <strong>setup minute overrides</strong> on estimates adjust one-off complexity without changing master data.</li>
                    <li>Planned minutes on <strong>jobs</strong> use the same defaults unless overridden at estimate time.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section" id="presses">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Presses</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/Presses/Index">Open presses</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    Press records define equipment capabilities and costing: web width, repeat range, speed,
                    setup time, and whether pricing is hourly or click-based.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Setup</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Enter <strong>name</strong>, <strong>code</strong>, and <strong>press type</strong> (e.g. digital toner).</li>
                    <li>Set <strong>web width</strong>, <strong>min/max repeat</strong>, and <strong>max colors</strong> for imposition validation.</li>
                    <li>Record <strong>speed (fpm)</strong> and <strong>setup minutes</strong> for run-time and makeready estimates.</li>
                    <li>Enter <strong>cost per hour</strong> for time-based presses, or enable <strong>click-based</strong> and configure inks separately.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Usage</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>One press is selected per estimate line; drives impression count, press minutes, and click cost.</li>
                    <li>Imposition checks label layout against press web width and repeat limits.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section docs-section--workflows" id="estimating">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Estimating</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/Estimates/Index">Open estimates</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    Estimates are the central quoting object. Each estimate has a customer, one or more lines
                    (label specs), quantity breaks, and a calculated cost breakdown before markup and sale price.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Prerequisites</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>At least one <strong>customer</strong>, <strong>stock</strong>, and <strong>press</strong> must exist.</li>
                    <li>Add <strong>inks</strong> and <strong>finishing operations</strong> before quoting jobs that need them.</li>
                    <li>Optional: link a <strong>die</strong> for fixed layouts; otherwise set label dimensions and imposition manually.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Creating an estimate</h3>
                <ol class="docs-list docs-list--ordered">
                    <li>Go to <strong>Sales → Estimates</strong> and create a new draft.</li>
                    <li>Select the <strong>customer</strong> and add one or more <strong>lines</strong> with label size, stock, and press.</li>
                    <li>Enter <strong>quantity breaks</strong> (e.g. 1,000 / 5,000 / 10,000 labels).</li>
                    <li>Choose <strong>inks</strong>, <strong>finishing operations</strong>, and waste assumptions (setup waste impressions, etc.).</li>
                    <li>Use <strong>Calculate</strong> to run the engine and review imposition diagram and cost breakdown per quantity.</li>
                    <li>Apply markup and save; convert to a <strong>product</strong> or <strong>sales order</strong> when the quote is accepted.</li>
                </ol>

                <div class="docs-callout">
                    <strong>Imposition</strong>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        The calculator lays out labels across the press web, computes impressions and linear feet,
                        and rolls up material, press, ink click, and finishing costs. Override fields on a line
                        (orientation, labels across/around) when you need a manual layout different from the die default.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section docs-section--workflows" id="purchase-orders">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Purchase orders</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/PurchaseOrders/Index">Open purchase orders</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    Purchase orders (POs) order stock from a supplier. A PO starts as a <strong>draft</strong>,
                    is <strong>sent</strong> to the vendor, and is later received against inventory.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Creating &amp; editing</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Pick the <strong>supplier</strong> first. Each line's <strong>Stock</strong> dropdown is then filtered to that supplier's stock, with a per-line <strong>Stock type</strong> filter to narrow it further.</li>
                    <li>Add as many lines as needed with <strong>+ Add line</strong>; use <strong>Remove</strong> to drop a line.</li>
                    <li>Enter the <strong>quantity (LF)</strong>. <strong>Unit cost is derived automatically</strong> from the stock's cost per MSI and roll width — it is read-only and shown with a live line total. You no longer type a unit price.</li>
                    <li>Set the <strong>expected date</strong> and <strong>notes</strong>. On a draft you can edit these, add/remove lines, then <strong>Save</strong> (saving re-derives unit costs from current stock costs).</li>
                    <li><strong>Delete draft</strong> removes a draft PO entirely.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">View &amp; send</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>View PO</strong> opens the purchase order as a PDF in a new tab, using your company name and logo from <a asp-page="/Settings/General">General settings</a>. It is available at any status.</li>
                    <li><strong>Send PO</strong> marks the PO sent and can email the PO PDF to the vendor. The recipient dropdown lists the supplier's <strong>contact emails</strong> (primary first); if none are on file the PO is marked sent without emailing.</li>
                    <li>Email delivery requires Mailgun to be configured under <a asp-page="/Settings/Email">Setup → Email</a>; otherwise the send is logged and skipped.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="docs-callout docs-callout--tip">
                    <strong>Cost basis</strong>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        Line cost per linear foot is computed as <em>cost&nbsp;per&nbsp;MSI × (12 × roll&nbsp;width) ÷ 1000</em>.
                        Keep each stock's <strong>cost per MSI</strong> and <strong>width</strong> current so PO pricing stays accurate.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section docs-section--workflows" id="shipping">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Shipping &amp; fulfillment</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/Production/Shipping">Open shipping</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    <strong>Production → Shipping</strong> is the shipping workspace. It lists sales orders that have
                    items finished and ready to ship (jobs at the <strong>Rewound</strong> stage), each with an
                    <strong>“N of M items ready”</strong> badge. Recording a shipment here captures packages and tracking
                    and posts the shipment to the shipments list.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Prerequisites</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Jobs must progress through production to the <strong>Rewound</strong> (ready-to-ship) stage to appear here.</li>
                    <li>The <strong>ship-from address</strong> is taken from your company address in <a asp-page="/Settings/General">General settings</a> — set it before shipping.</li>
                    <li>The order's customer needs a <strong>shipping address</strong> for the ship-to selection.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Recording a shipment</h3>
                <ol class="docs-list docs-list--ordered">
                    <li>On the Shipping workspace, click <strong>Record shipment</strong> for an order.</li>
                    <li>Set the <strong>ship date</strong>, <strong>carrier</strong> (FedEx, UPS, USPS, DHL, or Other), and <strong>ship-to</strong> address.</li>
                    <li>Check the <strong>items</strong> to ship and the quantity for each. Partial shipments are allowed — ship what's ready and the rest of the order stays open.</li>
                    <li>Add one or more <strong>packages</strong> with weight, dimensions, declared value, <strong>tracking number</strong>, and cost. Every package needs a tracking number.</li>
                    <li>Click <strong>Record shipment</strong>. The shipment is created as <strong>In transit</strong>, the shipped jobs advance to <strong>Shipped</strong>, and the sales order rolls up to Shipped once all lines are out.</li>
                </ol>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Mark shipped / picked up (no packages)</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>For hand-offs with no packages or tracking, use <strong>Mark shipped</strong> on the Ready to Ship tab — it creates a package-less shipment for all ready items, advances the jobs, and rolls up the order.</li>
                    <li>Orders on a <strong>pickup</strong> shipping method show <strong>Mark picked up</strong> instead.</li>
                    <li>The button is disabled until <strong>every item on the order is ready</strong>; partial orders go through the Record shipment form. An "Unmark" is not needed — the shipment appears in history like any other.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Usage</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Recorded shipments appear under <a asp-page="/Shipments/Index">Shipping → all shipments</a> and on each shipment's detail page (ship-from/ship-to, carrier, packages, and tracking).</li>
                    <li>Tracking numbers are <strong>entered manually</strong>; there is no live carrier label generation.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="docs-callout">
                    <strong>Ship-from address</strong>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        The company address from General settings is snapshotted onto each shipment at the time it is
                        recorded, so later edits to your company address don't change historical shipments.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section docs-section--workflows" id="invoicing">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Invoicing</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/Invoices/Index">Open invoices</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    Invoices are generated <strong>from a shipment</strong>. An order appears on the Invoices page only
                    after an invoice has been created — shipping an order does not invoice it automatically.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Generating an invoice</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Open a shipment that is <strong>In transit</strong> or <strong>Delivered</strong> and click <strong>Generate invoice</strong> (Admin or Accounting roles).</li>
                    <li>The invoice copies the shipped lines, quantities, and unit prices from the sales order. Tax is applied at the configured rate unless the customer is <strong>tax exempt</strong>; shipping cost is carried over.</li>
                    <li>Due date is calculated from the customer's payment <strong>terms</strong>.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Usage</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>The invoices page is split into <strong>Ready to export</strong> and <strong>Exported</strong> tabs; filter by status, customer, date, or AR aging bucket within either.</li>
                    <li>Check invoices and <strong>Export to QuickBooks</strong> (downloads the CSV and marks them exported, with who/when recorded) or <strong>Mark selected as exported</strong> without a download; <strong>Unmark exported</strong> reverses a mistake.</li>
                    <li>Record <strong>payments</strong> and <strong>void</strong> invoices from the invoice detail page.</li>
                    <li>The <strong>sales tax rate</strong> applied to new invoices is set in <a asp-page="/Settings/General">General settings</a>; tax-exempt customers are never taxed.</li>
                    <li>Access to all invoice pages requires the <strong>Accounting</strong> role.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="docs-section docs-section--workflows" id="finance-reports">
                <div class="docs-section-header">
                    <h2 class="docs-section-title">Finance &amp; reports</h2>
                    <a class="docs-section-action" asp-page="/Reports/Index">Open finance reports</a>
                </div>
                <p>
                    The Finance menu is visible only to users with the <strong>Accounting</strong> role
                    (assigned under Setup → Users, combinable with any other roles). The shop dashboard itself
                    carries no financial data — it stays operational for the floor.
                </p>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Finance reports</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li><strong>Widgets</strong> — revenue this month, receivables, open order value, invoiced revenue trend, AR aging, order intake, and top customers, with a 30-day to 12-month range selector and customizable layout.</li>
                    <li><strong>Report generator</strong> — pick an area (invoices, payments, sales orders, estimates, jobs, shipments, purchase orders), a date range, and optional customer/product/die/supplier filters; view the table on screen or <strong>Download CSV</strong>.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="docs-subtitle">Estimate vs Actual</h3>
                <ul class="docs-list">
                    <li>Compares each job's <strong>quoted cost</strong> (from its estimate quantity break) against <strong>actual cost</strong> (clocked labor × equipment rate + recorded material usage), alongside revenue and margins.</li>
                    <li>Summary tiles, variance and lowest-margin charts, and a per-job table with variance highlighted; filter by date, customer, and finished-only; export to CSV.</li>
                    <li>Accuracy depends on the floor recording counts, clocking time, and scanning rolls — jobs with nothing recorded show near-zero actual cost.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@section Scripts {
    <script>
        (function () {
            var links = document.querySelectorAll('.docs-nav-link');
            var sections = [];
            links.forEach(function (link) {
                var id = link.getAttribute('href').slice(1);
                var el = document.getElementById(id);
                if (el) sections.push({ link: link, el: el });
            });

            function updateActive() {
                var scrollY = window.scrollY + 120;
                var current = sections[0];
                sections.forEach(function (s) {
                    if (s.el.offsetTop <= scrollY) current = s;
                });
                links.forEach(function (l) { l.classList.remove('is-active'); });
                if (current) current.link.classList.add('is-active');
            }

            window.addEventListener('scroll', updateActive, { passive: true });
            updateActive();
        })();
    </script>
}
